-- Seed the review agenda: one "About this model" statement and 12 to 13 review questions in each of the four synthetic spaces.
--
-- Why. Expert review happens inside the spaces. A reviewer who opens a space with no prompt has to audit a library. A short, typed
-- agenda lets an expert spend about two hours correcting a drafted structure. Each question is a discussion topic that points at one
-- exact part or concept, and carries one of five types taken from the lab's own theory records: aim and theory, boundary, order of
-- steps, sources, how progress shows. The "About this model" topic states what the model is for, what is held fixed, what varies and
-- what it can and cannot say (design note section 6.2, items X16 and X17).
--
-- What it does. For each of the four spaces it adds discussion topics, resolved by project slug, part title and concept label. It
-- stops with an error if any link target is missing or matches more than one record. It skips a topic that already exists with the
-- same title in the same space, so it is safe to run twice. It adds no review assignments, because no reviewer is named yet and an
-- unassigned assignment would show as "under review". The Discussions page lists newest first, so each row gets a timestamp one second
-- earlier than the row before it. The "About this model" statement then comes first and the questions follow in order.
--
-- Read-only check on 2026-10-28: all 50 link targets resolve to exactly one record. 54 topics would be added.
--
-- Source: docs/design-notes/openlpm-four-space-review-agenda-and-model-aims-2026-10-28.md (lab_manager).
-- DO NOT APPLY until Dustin has approved the agenda. Applying it is a production database write.

SET search_path = public, extensions;

DO $MIGRATION$
DECLARE
  v_rows JSONB := $q$[{"space":"bio-core-lpm-synth","category":"About this model","title":"About this model: Bio-Core","description":"What this model is for. Bio-Core is one half of a controlled contrast with the Interdisciplinary space. It shows what happens to a curriculum for evolution when the explanation uses population-level words only and leaves out organism agency. The contrast helps us discuss how an assumption changes the structure of the curriculum: which concepts exist, how widely each is defined, how concepts attach to topics, how connected the topic space is and how evenly weight is spread.\n\nHeld fixed with the Interdisciplinary space: the drafting lab and method, the format (strands by four grade bands, with performance indicators), and the aim of showing a progression for evolution.\nVaries: the vocabulary (narrow, agency excluded, biology only), the size (12 parts and 25 concept entries, against 16 parts and 244 entries), and the number of subject areas (1, against 4). Measures that depend on size have to be corrected for size before they are compared.\n\nWhat it can say: how these choices change the structure. What it cannot say: which model is right, or whether students learn differently with one of them. No class has used either.\n\nDisclosures. The lab wrote both models with AI help. The theory behind this space (dichotomized causal reasoning) is the lab's reconstruction of a published position and has not been checked by the people who hold it. The lab also wrote one of the two assessment lines of work that the comparison cites. No subject expert has reviewed this space.\n\nAI-drafted statement. No expert has checked it. If it misdescribes the model, say so.","kind":null,"anchor":null},{"space":"bio-core-lpm-synth","category":"Aim and theory","title":"Aim and theory: The Four-Component Natural Selection Mechanism","description":"The space states one aim: explain evolution with population-level words only, with no organism agency. The theory behind that aim (dichotomized causal reasoning) is the lab's own reconstruction of a published position. Does the content of this part follow from the aim? Would someone who holds the position recognise it?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"The Four-Component Natural Selection Mechanism"},{"space":"bio-core-lpm-synth","category":"Aim and theory","title":"Aim and theory: Organisms and Their Habitats","description":"The space follows a multi-year trajectory and also names the German Basiskonzept tradition. Does starting at kindergarten with organisms and their habitats fit the trajectory? Would the Basiskonzept tradition start somewhere else?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Organisms and Their Habitats"},{"space":"bio-core-lpm-synth","category":"Aim and theory","title":"Aim and theory: Adaptation","description":"Adaptation is a main concept at K-2, 3-5 and 9-12 and is absent at 6-8. Does the aim explain that pattern, or is it a gap?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"concept","anchor":"Adaptation"},{"space":"bio-core-lpm-synth","category":"Boundary","title":"Boundary: Evaluating Adaptationist Claims","description":"The space leaves out organism agency. At 9-12 students judge claims that a trait is \"for\" something. Where would a teacher or student say \"the organism chose\", and what should this part do with that kind of talk?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Evaluating Adaptationist Claims"},{"space":"bio-core-lpm-synth","category":"Boundary","title":"Boundary: Signal","description":"Information, Communication and Signal appear in stations and the concept map, and in no part's concept list. Do they belong inside the narrow boundary, or do they belong to another space?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"concept","anchor":"Signal"},{"space":"bio-core-lpm-synth","category":"Boundary","title":"Boundary: Population","description":"Which idea from the German Basiskonzept tradition has no counterpart in this space's concepts, and does the boundary rule explain why?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"concept","anchor":"Population"},{"space":"bio-core-lpm-synth","category":"Order of steps","title":"Order of steps: Evidence, Deep Time, and Patterns of Extinction","description":"Only this part among the 12 states a prerequisite (Evaluating Adaptationist Claims). Name the other pairs of parts where one must come before the other.\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Evidence, Deep Time, and Patterns of Extinction"},{"space":"bio-core-lpm-synth","category":"Order of steps","title":"Order of steps: Genes, Alleles, and the Origin of Variation","description":"Selection (B1) and variation (B2) are taught side by side. Should students meet variation and inheritance before the selection mechanism, or after?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Genes, Alleles, and the Origin of Variation"},{"space":"bio-core-lpm-synth","category":"Order of steps","title":"Order of steps: Speciation","description":"Speciation is a main concept in one part (6-8) and does not return. Where would a biology teacher revisit it?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"concept","anchor":"Speciation"},{"space":"bio-core-lpm-synth","category":"Sources","title":"Sources: How Populations Become New Species","description":"No part of the space cites a source. Name the paper, curriculum document or textbook a teacher would check this part against. The apple maggot fly station on this part describes host races (groups that can still interbreed) and may read as a finished species split.\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"How Populations Become New Species"},{"space":"bio-core-lpm-synth","category":"Sources","title":"Sources: Trait Advantage and Simple Selection Scenarios","description":"Which research on student ideas about selection (for example the idea that a need makes a trait appear) supports the choices in this part?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Trait Advantage and Simple Selection Scenarios"},{"space":"bio-core-lpm-synth","category":"How progress shows","title":"How progress shows: Species, Relatedness, and Extinction","description":"What would a 3-5 student say or do that shows they hold the idea in this part, and what would show a common misconception? Do the three indicators describe things a teacher can observe?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Species, Relatedness, and Extinction"},{"space":"bio-core-lpm-synth","category":"How progress shows","title":"How progress shows: Population-Level Genetics and Allele Frequency","description":"At 9-12, what task would show that a student reasons about allele frequency in a population and has not slipped into reasoning about one organism?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Population-Level Genetics and Allele Frequency"},{"space":"social-core-lpm-synth","category":"About this model","title":"About this model: Social-Core","description":"What this model is for. Social-Core is one of three narrow spaces that share one skeleton (3 strands of 4 parts, 15 concepts in parts). It shows what a vocabulary rule does when the subject changes and the skeleton stays the same. Its rule: an intentional and institutional vocabulary for groups, norms, cooperation, conflict and social change, with nothing borrowed from biology or computing.\n\nHeld fixed with the other narrow spaces: the skeleton, the drafting lab and method, the format. Varies: the subject and its vocabulary.\n\nWhat it can say: how a social-science vocabulary shapes which concepts lead which parts and how parts connect. What it cannot say: which social theory is right, or what students learn. The space states no theory yet, and asking reviewers to name one is part of the agenda.\n\nDisclosures. The lab wrote it with AI help on 2026-10-25. No social scientist has reviewed it and no equity review has taken place. The literature anchors were matched from titles and were not read in full. It is private.\n\nAI-drafted statement. No expert has checked it. If it misdescribes the model, say so.","kind":null,"anchor":null},{"space":"social-core-lpm-synth","category":"Aim and theory","title":"Aim and theory: Cooperation","description":"The space states no theory, only a vocabulary rule. Which theory or theories of cooperation and collective action should it follow? Name one that fits the aim and one that would change it.\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"concept","anchor":"Cooperation"},{"space":"social-core-lpm-synth","category":"Aim and theory","title":"Aim and theory: Shared-Resource Problems","description":"Cooperation is a main concept in K-2 and 3-5 and only a supporting concept from 6-8, yet the 6-8 and 9-12 parts are about shared-resource problems. Does the aim explain that, or should Cooperation stay main?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Shared-Resource Problems"},{"space":"social-core-lpm-synth","category":"Aim and theory","title":"Aim and theory: Agency","description":"The 9-12 change part uses Agency as a main concept. Is a vocabulary of intentions and institutions enough to explain large-scale social change, or does it leave out something a social scientist would add?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"concept","anchor":"Agency"},{"space":"social-core-lpm-synth","category":"Boundary","title":"Boundary: Norms","description":"The space allows no mechanism borrowed from biology or computing. Does that exclude ideas a social scientist uses, such as models of how norms spread? Is the rule too strict, too loose, or right?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"concept","anchor":"Norms"},{"space":"social-core-lpm-synth","category":"Boundary","title":"Boundary: Why Societies Differ","description":"The preparation note says Western and English-language sources dominate. Which regions, groups or traditions are missing from this part, and what would adding them change?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Why Societies Differ"},{"space":"social-core-lpm-synth","category":"Boundary","title":"Boundary: Culture","description":"Culture appears in several parts. Where does culture end and institutions begin in this space, and is the line the same in each band?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"concept","anchor":"Culture"},{"space":"social-core-lpm-synth","category":"Order of steps","title":"Order of steps: Formal Institutions and Informal Norms","description":"S1 is marked as foundational to S2, yet this S1 9-12 part lists an S2 6-8 part as a prerequisite. Which direction is right, or is the dependence two-way?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Formal Institutions and Informal Norms"},{"space":"social-core-lpm-synth","category":"Order of steps","title":"Order of steps: How a Practice Spreads","description":"The lower-band parts of the social change strand state no prerequisites. Should students meet spreading of practices before or after groups and rules?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"How a Practice Spreads"},{"space":"social-core-lpm-synth","category":"Sources","title":"Sources: How Norms Are Enforced","description":"The literature anchors were matched from titles and domains and \"not read in full\". Take the anchors listed for this part: do they support it? Which sources are missing?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"How Norms Are Enforced"},{"space":"social-core-lpm-synth","category":"Sources","title":"Sources: Power","description":"All 18 concept entries are marked proposed. Which would a social scientist accept as defined, and which need rewriting? Start with Power.\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"concept","anchor":"Power"},{"space":"social-core-lpm-synth","category":"How progress shows","title":"How progress shows: Sharing and Taking Turns","description":"What would a K-2 child do that shows the idea in this part? Do the three indicators describe things a teacher can see?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Sharing and Taking Turns"},{"space":"social-core-lpm-synth","category":"How progress shows","title":"How progress shows: Explaining Large-Scale Change","description":"What answer from a 9-12 student would show they can explain change with agency and institutions together, and what would show a one-cause answer?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Explaining Large-Scale Change"},{"space":"computational-core-lpm-synth","category":"About this model","title":"About this model: Computational-Core","description":"What this model is for. Computational-Core is one of three narrow spaces that share one skeleton (3 strands of 4 parts, 15 concepts in parts). It shows what a vocabulary rule does when the subject changes and the skeleton stays the same. Its rule: a procedural and formal vocabulary for data, algorithms, systems and models that credits no intention or understanding to machines.\n\nHeld fixed with the other narrow spaces: the skeleton, the drafting lab and method, the format. Varies: the subject and its vocabulary. It also carries a list of 76 AI4K12 entries as a second view of the same ground.\n\nWhat it can say: how a formal vocabulary shapes which concepts lead which parts, and where its order of steps meets the timing of an official curriculum. What it cannot say: which account of computing education is right, or what students learn. The space states no theory yet.\n\nDisclosures. The lab wrote it with AI help on 2026-10-25. No computing education expert has reviewed it. It is private.\n\nAI-drafted statement. No expert has checked it. If it misdescribes the model, say so.","kind":null,"anchor":null},{"space":"computational-core-lpm-synth","category":"Aim and theory","title":"Aim and theory: Abstraction","description":"Abstraction is a supporting concept in several parts and never the main concept of any part. Should it lead a part? In which band?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"concept","anchor":"Abstraction"},{"space":"computational-core-lpm-synth","category":"Aim and theory","title":"Aim and theory: Decomposition, Functions and Testing","description":"The space states no theory. Which account of computational thinking or computing education should it follow, and does this part fit that account?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Decomposition, Functions and Testing"},{"space":"computational-core-lpm-synth","category":"Aim and theory","title":"Aim and theory: Machine Learning, Search and Distributed Systems Compared","description":"The vocabulary rule credits no intention or understanding to machines. Does \"learning from data\" in this part keep to the rule? Where would the wording slip?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Machine Learning, Search and Distributed Systems Compared"},{"space":"computational-core-lpm-synth","category":"Boundary","title":"Boundary: Information","description":"Information is a computing idea here and also appears in the Bio-Core and Interdisciplinary spaces with other meanings. Should this space keep its meaning apart, and how would a student tell the meanings apart?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"concept","anchor":"Information"},{"space":"computational-core-lpm-synth","category":"Boundary","title":"Boundary: Search","description":"Machine Learning and Search each appear once, at 9-12. Is one appearance enough for a main idea of computing, or should they build up over earlier bands?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"concept","anchor":"Search"},{"space":"computational-core-lpm-synth","category":"Order of steps","title":"Order of steps: Agent-Based and Feedback Models","description":"Four of the space's 14 order claims conflict with Sachsen's own timing, since Sachsen starts computing in grade 7 and this space starts in kindergarten. Is the space too early or is Sachsen late? What do you base that on?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Agent-Based and Feedback Models"},{"space":"computational-core-lpm-synth","category":"Order of steps","title":"Order of steps: Sequences, Loops and Conditions","description":"This part is a prerequisite for the 6-8 models part. Is it needed before modelling, or can modelling come first with ready-made blocks?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Sequences, Loops and Conditions"},{"space":"computational-core-lpm-synth","category":"Order of steps","title":"Order of steps: Machines Follow Rules People Write","description":"The space starts computing in kindergarten. What supports that start, and what does it cost if it is wrong?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Machines Follow Rules People Write"},{"space":"computational-core-lpm-synth","category":"Sources","title":"Sources: Information, Compression and the Limits of Representation","description":"Name the source a teacher would check this part against. Is this too abstract for 9-12 as written?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Information, Compression and the Limits of Representation"},{"space":"computational-core-lpm-synth","category":"Sources","title":"Sources: Models and Simulations of Everyday Systems","description":"The space matches its parts to the AI4K12 list: 21 matches of which 3 are strong, and 28 items in focus with no stage. Which matches are right, and where should the 28 go?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Models and Simulations of Everyday Systems"},{"space":"computational-core-lpm-synth","category":"How progress shows","title":"How progress shows: Following and Writing Steps","description":"What would a K-2 child do that shows the idea in this part? Do the indicators describe things a teacher can see without a device?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Following and Writing Steps"},{"space":"computational-core-lpm-synth","category":"How progress shows","title":"How progress shows: Correctness and Efficiency","description":"What task would show a 9-12 student separates a program being correct from it being fast?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Correctness and Efficiency"},{"space":"interdisciplinary-lpm-synth","category":"About this model","title":"About this model: Interdisciplinary","description":"What this model is for. Interdisciplinary is the other half of the controlled contrast with Bio-Core. It shows what happens when the same shared ideas (information, adaptation, selection and others) are defined across several fields, each with a note on what does not carry over, and when agency is used where it fits. The contrast helps us discuss how assumptions change the structure of the curriculum: which concepts exist, how widely each is defined, how concepts attach to topics, how connected the topic space is and how evenly weight is spread.\n\nHeld fixed with Bio-Core: the drafting lab and method, the format (strands by four grade bands, with performance indicators), and the aim of showing a progression for evolution.\nVaries: the vocabulary (wide, a meaning per field, agency used where it fits), the size (16 parts and 244 concept entries, against 12 parts and 25 entries), and the number of subject areas (4, against 1). This space reaches more curriculum partly because its vocabulary is larger. Reach and similar measures have to be corrected for size before they are compared.\n\nWhat it can say: how these choices change the structure. What it cannot say: which model is right, or whether students learn differently with one of them. No class has used either.\n\nDisclosures. The lab wrote both models with AI help, and one of the two assessment lines of work that the comparison cites. No subject expert has reviewed this space.\n\nAI-drafted statement. No expert has checked it. If it misdescribes the model, say so.","kind":null,"anchor":null},{"space":"interdisciplinary-lpm-synth","category":"Aim and theory","title":"Aim and theory: Agency, Niche Construction, and Selection","description":"The space's stated theory is integrated causal reasoning. This part uses agency where it fits. Does the part follow from the theory, and would someone who holds the theory agree?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Agency, Niche Construction, and Selection"},{"space":"interdisciplinary-lpm-synth","category":"Aim and theory","title":"Aim and theory: Groups, Rules, and Tools","description":"The four strands share almost no vocabulary (average overlap 0.07, against 0.16 in the three narrow spaces). Is that the point of an interdisciplinary space, or is a bridging idea missing?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Groups, Rules, and Tools"},{"space":"interdisciplinary-lpm-synth","category":"Boundary","title":"Boundary: Agency","description":"The accepted Agency entry says a \"system\" can \"act intentionally\". The Computational-Core space forbids intention for machines. Read the entry: does it hold across biology, culture and technology, and what should its \"does not carry over\" note say?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"concept","anchor":"Agency"},{"space":"interdisciplinary-lpm-synth","category":"Boundary","title":"Boundary: Agency and Niche Construction Across Biological and Technological Systems","description":"The station \"Agency and Niche Construction Across Biological and Technological Systems\" treats biological and technological systems together. Where does that comparison stop being valid?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Agency and Niche Construction Across Biological and Technological Systems"},{"space":"interdisciplinary-lpm-synth","category":"Boundary","title":"Boundary: Meaning","description":"The Meaning entry names one account as \"unverified\". Which account of meaning belongs in a school progression, and what is the plain difference between meaning in biology and meaning in language?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"concept","anchor":"Meaning"},{"space":"interdisciplinary-lpm-synth","category":"Boundary","title":"Boundary: Signal","description":"A note on the Signal entry says two of its sources were not read. Read the entry and the sources: does the general definition hold, and are the \"does not carry over\" notes right?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"concept","anchor":"Signal"},{"space":"interdisciplinary-lpm-synth","category":"Boundary","title":"Boundary: Selection","description":"The \"does not carry over\" notes say where selection in biology differs from selection among ideas, in schools or in AI. Do the notes capture the main differences? What is missing?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"concept","anchor":"Selection"},{"space":"interdisciplinary-lpm-synth","category":"Order of steps","title":"Order of steps: The Shared Structure of Discovery: Darwin's Notebooks and the History of Science","description":"This part and the 6-8 part before it in strand I1 list the same two main concepts, Analogical Reasoning and Metacognition. Is that a deliberate spiral or a repeat? What should change between the two?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"The Shared Structure of Discovery: Darwin's Notebooks and the History of Science"},{"space":"interdisciplinary-lpm-synth","category":"Order of steps","title":"Order of steps: The General Pattern: Variation, Selection, Transmission","description":"No part states a prerequisite, so order relies on the band sequence and on links between strands. Which of the 16 parts need one, and which parts should come first?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"The General Pattern: Variation, Selection, Transmission"},{"space":"interdisciplinary-lpm-synth","category":"Sources","title":"Sources: Selection Across Biology, Culture, Education, and AI","description":"None of the 16 parts cites a source, and this one makes the widest claim. Name the sources a teacher would check it against.\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Selection Across Biology, Culture, Education, and AI"},{"space":"interdisciplinary-lpm-synth","category":"Sources","title":"Sources: Information","description":"The grounding field of most concept entries is a provenance statement and only 17 name a source. Pick three entries and say whether the definition matches the best source you know.\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"concept","anchor":"Information"},{"space":"interdisciplinary-lpm-synth","category":"How progress shows","title":"How progress shows: Comparing Biological and Cultural Transmission","description":"What would a 3-5 student say that shows they see how biological and cultural passing-on are alike and different? What would show they merged the two?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Comparing Biological and Cultural Transmission"},{"space":"interdisciplinary-lpm-synth","category":"How progress shows","title":"How progress shows: Collective Intelligence, Distributed Cognition, and Coevolving Technology","description":"At 9-12, what task would show a student can reason about groups and tools together without treating the group as a single mind?\n\nAI-drafted question. No expert wrote or checked it. Answer in a few sentences, or say the question is badly put.","kind":"part","anchor":"Collective Intelligence, Distributed Cognition, and Coevolving Technology"}]$q$::jsonb;
  r JSONB;
  v_project UUID;
  v_link UUID;
  v_n INTEGER;
  v_added INTEGER := 0;
  v_i INTEGER := 0;
BEGIN
  FOR r IN SELECT * FROM jsonb_array_elements(v_rows) LOOP
    SELECT id INTO v_project FROM projects WHERE slug = r ->> 'space';
    IF v_project IS NULL THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'Project % not found', r ->> 'space';
    END IF;

    v_link := NULL;
    IF r ->> 'kind' = 'part' THEN
      SELECT count(*), min(id::text)::uuid INTO v_n, v_link FROM lpm_data_objects WHERE project_id = v_project AND title = r ->> 'anchor';
      IF v_n <> 1 THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Part "%" in % matches % records', r ->> 'anchor', r ->> 'space', v_n; END IF;
    ELSIF r ->> 'kind' = 'concept' THEN
      SELECT count(*), min(id::text)::uuid INTO v_n, v_link FROM lpm_schema_elements WHERE project_id = v_project AND element_type = 'concept' AND label = r ->> 'anchor';
      IF v_n <> 1 THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Concept "%" in % matches % records', r ->> 'anchor', r ->> 'space', v_n; END IF;
    END IF;

    v_i := v_i + 1;
    IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM discussion_topics WHERE project_id = v_project AND title = r ->> 'title') THEN
      INSERT INTO discussion_topics (project_id, title, description, category, linked_type, linked_id, created_at, updated_at)
      VALUES (v_project, r ->> 'title', r ->> 'description', r ->> 'category',
              CASE r ->> 'kind' WHEN 'part' THEN 'data_object' WHEN 'concept' THEN 'schema_element' ELSE NULL END,
              v_link, now() - v_i * interval '1 second', now() - v_i * interval '1 second');
      v_added := v_added + 1;
    END IF;
  END LOOP;
  RAISE NOTICE 'Review agenda: % topics added (54 expected on first run)', v_added;
END
$MIGRATION$;
